 Standard persistent homology defined over a growing sequence of simplicial complexes is a fundamental tool in topological data analysis (TDA). Since the advent of persistence algorithm and its algebraic understanding, various extensions of the basic concept have been explored. Among these extensions, zigzag persistence introduced by Carlsson  and de Silva \cite{arxivID-0812.0197-0} is one of the most important extensions. In a nutshell, a zigzag filtration is a sequence of simplicial complexes that alternates between insertion and deletion of simplices. The theory and algorithm of zigzag persistence are much more sophisticated than those of standard persistence.

In the standard persistence, the barcode is a concise representation of the persistence diagram. However, in zigzag persistence, the barcode is not sufficient to describe the persistence diagram. To address this problem, the notion of \emph{zigzag barcode} was introduced by Carlsson and de Silva \cite{arxivID-0812.0197-1}. The zigzag barcode is a sequence of pairs, each of which is a pair of persistence diagrams. These persistence diagrams are in different dimensions and are arranged in a way such that the persistence diagram of one dimension is a subdiagram of the next one. As a result, the zigzag barcode is a multi-dimensional object. The zigzag barcode of a zigzag filtration can be computed using the algorithm proposed by Carlsson and de Silva \cite{arxivID-0812.0197-2}. However, this algorithm is relatively complicated and has a high computational complexity. In \cite{arxivID-1604.00655-3}, the zigzag barcode was interpreted as a 2-parameter persistence module, and a stability result for zigzag persistence was proved using the stability of 2-parameter persistence. In \cite{arxivID-1805.11190-4}, the authors defined a metric on the space of zigzag modules, which is called the reflection distance. They proved that this metric is an upper bound for the $\ell^1$-bottleneck distance between zigzag barcodes.

