The zigzag persistence algorithm proposed by Carlsson and de Silva \cite{arxivID-0812.0197-5} computes the zigzag barcode of a zigzag filtration using the bottleneck distance between the persistence diagrams of successive complexes. This algorithm works in a similar way as the standard persistence algorithm but involves a more complicated bottleneck distance computation. In \cite{arxivID-2110.06315-6}, the authors proposed an algorithm to compute the zigzag barcode of a non-repetitive zigzag filtration. This algorithm is based on the fact that a non-repetitive zigzag filtration admits an up-down filtration as its canonical form. Therefore, the zigzag barcode of a non-repetitive zigzag filtration can be computed by computing the barcode of the corresponding up-down filtration. However, this algorithm is limited to non-repetitive zigzag filtrations. In \cite{arxivID-1911.10693-7}, the authors proposed a method to compute the zigzag barcode of a zigzag filtration using matrix factorization. This method is based on finding a canonical form of a matrix associated with a type A quiver representation, which in turn can be computed using factorizations of associated matrices. In \cite{arxivID-1608.06039-8}, the authors proposed an algorithm to compute the zigzag barcode of a zigzag filtration using zigzag persistent cohomology. This algorithm is based on the dualization of reflections and transpositions transformations within the zigzag sequence. In \cite{arxivID-2204.11080-9}, the authors proposed an algorithm to compute the zigzag barcode of a zigzag filtration using a conversion to a cell-wise non-zigzag filtration. This algorithm is based on the fact that a zigzag filtration can be converted to a cell-wise non-zigzag filtration with the same length, where the cells are copies of the input simplices. The barcode of the original filtration can be easily read from the barcode of the new cell-wise filtration because the conversion embodies a series of diamond switches known in topological data analysis.

In this paper, we propose an algorithm called FastZigzag to compute the zigzag barcode of a zigzag filtration. This algorithm is based on the fact that a zigzag filtration can be converted to a cell-wise non-zigzag filtration with the same length, where the cells are copies of the input simplices. The barcode of the original filtration can be easily read from the barcode of the new cell-wise filtration because the conversion embodies a series of diamond switches known in topological data analysis. This algorithm is simple and efficient. We implemented this algorithm in Python using the software library Ripser \cite{arxivID-1908.02518-10}. We tested this algorithm on a variety of datasets. The results show that this algorithm is faster and more memory efficient than the existing state-of-the-art softwares.

