 Transformer models have gained prominence in NLP research due to their powerful performances on leaderboards. However, numerous studies have shown these neural models are brittle, frequently taking shortcuts to reach decisions rather than reasoning about the underlying semantics correctly  #ref12# #ref4# or failing when exposed to adversarial perturbations of inputs  #ref14# #ref44# #ref19# #ref13# #ref7#. Due to the opaque nature of neural modeling, methods for adversarial example generation may  offer insights into the underlying decision-making processes of these models.

Adversarial examples are inputs to the model that have been intentionally perturbed to cause a model to make a mistake. Adversarial example generation is a well-studied problem in the vision community \cite{arxivID-1412.6572-0, arxivID-1608.04644-1, arxivID-1711.02846-2}. While there has been considerable work in the area of adversarial example generation for NLP \cite{arxivID-1801.07175-3, arxivID-1712.05419-4, arxivID-2107.13541-5, arxivID-2005.05909-6, arxivID-2010.05736-7}, the methods proposed in prior work suffer from several limitations. In particular, they are often unrealistic and diverge from the real-world data distributions. As a result, the adversarial examples generated by these methods may not generalize well to real-world applications.

In this work, we propose a two-stage adversarial example generation framework (NaturalAdversaries) for designing adversaries that are effective at fooling a given classifier and demonstrate natural-looking failure cases that could plausibly occur during in-the-wild deployment of the models. At the first stage, we use a token attribution method to summarize a given classifier's behaviour as a function of the key tokens in the input. In the second stage, we use a generative model to generate natural text that is conditioned on the key tokens from the first stage.

We evaluate our approach on two datasets and show that our method is able to generate adversarial examples that are both effective at fooling the classifier and natural-looking. Our results indicate that our method is adaptable to both black-box and white-box adversarial attacks based on the level of access to the model parameters. We also show that our method is able to generalize across domains, and offer insights for future research on improving the robustness of neural text classification models.

