 From infancy, humans can recognize when something unexpected happens in the physical world  #ref2#. When expectations are not met in a virtual setting, such as a game or Virtual Reality (VR) experience, user experience will be impacted. As technologies develop rapidly to support interactive experiences that encompass both the virtual and the  physical world, it is important to consider how users perceive the realism of these experiences. In this paper, we focus on the perception of animated throwing motions, which is an important part of many interactive applications.

To create realistic throwing motions, animators need to understand the perception of throwing. One important factor that affects perception is the Point of Release (PoR) of a ball. The PoR is the point in time when the ball is released from the hand. In this paper, we define early PoR as when the ball is released before it reaches the apex of the throw, and late PoR as when the ball is released after it reaches the apex of the throw. Figure \ref{fig:poR} shows examples of early and late PoR for overarm and underarm throws.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{figs/poR.pdf}
\caption{Examples of early and late PoR for overarm and underarm throws.}
\label{fig:poR}
\end{figure}

We conducted a perceptual study to investigate how the PoR affects the perception of throwing motions. We found that errors in overarm throws with a late PoR are detected more easily than an early PoR, while the opposite is true for underarm throws. We also found that the viewpoint and the distance the ball travels have an effect on perceived realism. These results can help improve the plausibility of throwing animations in interactive applications such as games or VR.
Related Work
There has been research on how humans perceive motion. One aspect of motion perception is the perception of velocity. Velocity is an important factor in the perception of throwing motions, as it affects how fast the ball moves through the air. Previous studies have shown that humans can accurately perceive the velocity of moving objects \cite{arxivID-1405.0545-0, arxivID-0911.4385-1}. 

Another aspect of motion perception is the perception of motion direction. Previous studies have shown that humans can accurately perceive the direction of moving objects \cite{arxivID-1801.06976-2, arxivID-1801.06687-3}. 

In addition to velocity and direction, the perception of motion is also affected by other factors, such as the size and shape of the moving object \cite{arxivID-1111.6563-4}. For example, humans tend to perceive larger objects as moving faster than smaller objects \cite{arxivID-1111.6563-5}. 

The perception of motion is also affected by the context in which the motion occurs. For example, humans can perceive the motion of objects in a scene more accurately when the objects are in a familiar environment \cite{arxivID-1911.07349-6}. This is because the context provides additional information about the scene and the objects within it, which can help humans make more accurate judgments about the motion of the objects. 

In summary, the perception of motion is a complex process that is affected by many factors, including velocity, direction, size, shape, and context. Understanding how these factors affect motion perception can help animators create more realistic and believable animations for virtual environments. <|section_end|>

