 Driving has seen its numerous phases of evolution, from being steam-propelled to becoming almost fully autonomous. Throughout these developments, the existence of one phenomenon has remained constant in the daily environment  intense sunlight which can obstruct the view of a vision sensor (either eyes or cameras) while maneuvering a vehicle. When the sun descends on the horizon, sun glare seeps below a cars  windshield and can dazzle the driver. This is a significant contributor to road accidents. According to the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA), glare is the second most prominent environmental reason for crashes, trailing only by nighttime driving. In addition, a study by the Insurance Institute for Highway Safety (IIHS) found that 12\% of all fatal crashes, and 7\% of all police-reported crashes, occur at dusk or at night. The combination of these factors makes sun glare a significant hazard in driving safety.

