 Motion forecasting is an essential pillar for the successful deployment of autonomous systems in environments comprising various heterogeneous agents. It presents the challenges of modeling (i) universal etiquette (e.g., goal-directed behaviors, avoiding collisions) that govern general motion dynamics of all agents; and (ii) social norms (e.g., the minimum separation distance, preferred speed) that influence the navigation styles of different agents across different locations. Owing to the success of deep neural networks on large-scale datasets, learning prediction models in a data-driven manner  has emerged as a promising approach for addressing the first challenge \cite{arxivID-2110.10436-0, arxivID-2307.07527-1, arxivID-2308.06419-2}. However, such models often suffer from a lack of generalization to new domains, such as unseen agent types and scene contexts \cite{arxivID-2201.12626-3, arxivID-2208.03578-4}. This is due to the domain shift between the training data and the new domain, which is often caused by the motion style shift of agents and/or scene context. To address this challenge, we propose a transfer learning approach that efficiently adapts pre-trained forecasting models to new domains.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{figs/teaser.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Motion style transfer.} We aim to transfer a pre-trained motion forecasting model to a new domain, such as unseen agent types and scene contexts. We introduce a low-rank motion style adapter that projects and adjusts the style features at a low-dimensional bottleneck. Additionally, we propose a modular adapter strategy that disentangles the features of scene context and motion history to facilitate a fine-grained choice of adaptation layers.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure}

A straightforward way to address the domain shift is to fine-tune the pre-trained model on the new domain. However, it is often impractical to update the entire model due to limited training data in the new domain. For example, if the model is pre-trained on a large-scale dataset and then fine-tuned on a small-scale dataset, the fine-tuned model can easily overfit to the new domain. To mitigate this issue, various parameter-efficient tuning (PETuning) methods have been proposed to update a small number of parameters, such as LoRA \cite{arxivID-2106.09685-5}, Adapter \cite{arxivID-1903.06930-6}, and prompt tuning \cite{arxivID-2104.08691-7}. However, these methods are originally designed for natural language processing (NLP) tasks and may not be suitable for motion forecasting tasks. For example, LoRA \cite{arxivID-2106.09685-8}updates the model weights by multiplying the low-rank matrices to the model weights, which requires the model weights to be updated during inference. This is not suitable for motion forecasting tasks, where the model weights are usually frozen after training. Adapter \cite{arxivID-1903.06930-9}and prompt tuning \cite{arxivID-2104.08691-10}are more suitable for motion forecasting tasks, but they still have limitations. Adapter \cite{arxivID-1903.06930-11}updates all layers of the model, which may be unnecessary for motion forecasting tasks. Prompt tuning \cite{arxivID-2104.08691-12}updates the input prompt, which may not be sufficient to capture the motion style shift.

