In this paper, we propose a novel data-driven approach to compute interpretable triage guidelines. We consider the problem of ventilator allocation for COVID-19 patients as a motivating application. Ventilators are critical resources for COVID-19 patients and are limited during a pandemic. The shortage of ventilators during the initial stages of the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States resulted in a large number of deaths, and policy makers had to develop guidelines to triage ventilator allocations. However, the official guidelines developed by the New York State (NYS) Department of Health in 2015 (before the COVID-19 pandemic) were found to be ineffective during the COVID-19 pandemic \cite{arxivID-2203.13506-0, arxivID-2203.13506-1}. In this work, we propose a novel data-driven approach to compute interpretable ventilator triage guidelines that can be used by policy makers and providers during a pandemic.

We formulate the ventilator allocation problem as a Markov decision process (MDP), where the goal is to maximize the expected number of lives saved. The optimal policy for this MDP is typically a complex function, making it difficult to interpret and use during a pandemic. We develop a novel approach to compute a policy that can be interpreted as a sequence of decision trees, referred to as a tree policy. We characterize the properties of tree policies and develop an algorithm based on dynamic programming recursions to compute a tree policy. We then use real patient data from Montefiore hospitals to compute a tree policy and use it to obtain novel triage guidelines for ventilator allocations for COVID-19 patients. We compare our guidelines with the official NYS guidelines and show that our guidelines are able to reduce the number of excess deaths associated with ventilator shortages. Our work highlights the limitations of the existing official triage guidelines, which need to be adapted specifically to COVID-19 before being successfully deployed.

