Interpretable machine learning has gained popularity in recent years, and a number of methods have been proposed to explain the predictions of machine learning models \cite{arxivID-2202.10335-5}. These methods can be broadly classified into two categories: post-hoc methods and intrinsic methods. Post-hoc methods are applied after a model has been trained, while intrinsic methods are built into the model during training. Post-hoc methods include feature importance, local interpretable model-agnostic explanations (LIME), and counterfactual explanations. Intrinsic methods include decision trees, decision rules, and rule lists. We use decision trees as an intrinsic method to compute interpretable triage guidelines. Decision trees are a popular method for classification and regression problems, and have been used in a number of applications \cite{arxivID-1305.7331-6, arxivID-1504.02018-7}. Decision trees are easy to interpret, and can be used to explain the predictions of more complex models. We use decision trees to compute interpretable triage guidelines, which can be easily understood by policy makers and providers. <|section_end|>

