 Automatic speaker verification (ASV)  #ref1# #ref2# is the task of verifying a persons identity using his or her voice. Modern ASV systems consists of three main components: acoustic feature extractor, speaker embedding extractor, and back-end classifier. In recent years, substantial improvement has been achieved by using deep  neural network (DNN) in both the feature extractor and the embedding extractor \cite{arxivID-1705.03670-0, arxivID-2001.04584-1, arxivID-1906.08333-2}. The feature extractor transforms the raw acoustic features into a more compact representation, such as Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients (MFCCs) \cite{arxivID-1003.4083-3}, filter banks (FBanks) \cite{arxivID-1702.03791-4}, or linear prediction cepstral coefficients (LPCCs) \cite{arxivID-1406.6473-5}, while the embedding extractor learns a discriminative speaker representation from the extracted features.

Most of the existing ASV systems use linear features, such as MFCCs, FBanks, and LPCCs. Although these features are widely used, they are not optimal for speaker recognition. To improve the performance of ASV, several nonlinear feature extraction methods have been proposed. For example, the power function was used in \cite{arxivID-1812.02447-6} to extract pitch-synchronous discrete cosine transform (PS-DCT) features for speaker identification. The power function was also used in \cite{arxivID-2204.02609-7} to extract nonlinear prediction features for speaker identification. In \cite{arxivID-1406.7314-8}, the authors compared the performance of several nonlinear features, including perceptual linear prediction (PLP) \cite{arxivID-1406.6473-9}, linear prediction cepstral coefficients (LPCCs), and PLP, for text-independent speaker verification. The results showed that PLP features outperformed MFCCs in both linear and nonlinear conditions. In \cite{arxivID-2203.03190-10}, the authors combined linear and nonlinear predictive models with classical LPCC parameterization for speaker recognition. The results showed that the combination of both models gave rise to an improvement over the classical method that considered only the LPCC coefficients. In \cite{arxivID-1808.00959-11}, the authors proposed a novel text-independent speaker identification method that used MFCCs and their dynamic information as feature sets to capture speaker characteristics. In \cite{arxivID-1706.09716-12}, the authors proposed second-order circular hidden Markov models to enhance the performance of isolated-word text-dependent speaker identification systems under shouted talking condition. In \cite{arxivID-2203.00513-13}, the authors compared the performance of several parameterizations, including MFCCs, PLP, and LPCCs, for speaker verification and identification tasks under different mismatch conditions. The results showed that the combination of several parameterizations can improve the robustness in all scenarios for both tasks. In \cite{arxivID-1702.00956-14}, the authors developed a speaker recognition system for NIST 2016 Speaker Recognition Evaluation. The system used several parameterizations, including MFCCs, PLP, and LPCCs, for speaker verification and identification tasks under different mismatch conditions. The results showed that the combination of several parameterizations can improve the robustness in all scenarios for both tasks.

