 Political polarization in America is growing more severe #ref51#, with a primacy of partyism and strong negative emotions increasingly characterizing inter-group discourse #ref65#. This affective polarization #ref35# is particularly concerning because partisan affect often only weakly correlates with actual policy preferences #ref34#. For these reasons, affective polarization poses a danger to the democratic process because partisan voters are  more likely to vote along party lines regardless of the candidates' policy positions, and thus parties have little incentive to reach out to the opposing group. As a result, political discourse becomes more hostile, and the public sphere becomes more fractured. This is further exacerbated by the echo chamber effect of social media, where people are more likely to encounter like-minded individuals, leading to an increase in partisan affect #ref65#.

A large body of social science research has focused on understanding the causes of affective polarization, and the potential effects of various interventions. \cite{arxivID-2103.06492-0, arxivID-2403.16940-1, arxivID-2110.11981-2}. In this paper, we focus on one intervention that has shown promise in reducing affective polarization: perspective-taking, or the practice of imagining how a situation may appear to another person. Perspective-taking is a well-established technique in psychology to reduce inter-group animosity #ref54# \cite{arxivID-2110.05596-3}. \cite{arxivID-2110.05596-4}. \cite{arxivID-2110.05596-5, arxivID-2111.13782-6, arxivID-2404.02277-7, arxivID-2205.11795-8, arxivID-1601.07264-9}. In this paper, we explore how perspective-taking can be implemented on social media platforms to reduce affective polarization.

