 Recent vision-language models (VLM) are predominantly build on pre-trained (foundational) large language models (LM)  #ref26# #ref3# #ref27# #ref10# #ref18#. As both  #ref3# and  #ref10# argue, this reduces the reliance on noisy vision web data while enabling a wider range of downstream tasks. However, while LMs show remarkable capabilities on tasks requiring commonsense knowledge  #ref24#, transferring these capabilities with standard  supervised fine-tuning to VLMs is challenging \cite{arxivID-2109.10246-0}. This is because the vision modality is not grounded in the language modality, which makes it difficult to effectively transfer the pre-trained knowledge to the VLM \cite{arxivID-2109.10246-1}. Consequently, VLMs are prone to hallucinate, e.g., generating an answer without providing a valid rationale, or providing a rationale that is not relevant to the given image.

To address this issue, we propose an iterative sampling and tuning paradigm, called \textbf{Illume}, that executes the following loop: Given an image-question-answer prompt, the VLM samples multiple candidate rationales, and a human critic provides minimal feedback via preference selection, used for fine-tuning. This loop increases the training data and gradually carves out the VLMs rationalization capabilities. In this work, we evaluate Illume on the image captioning task, where the goal is to generate a caption that describes the content of a given image.

Our experiments demonstrate that Illume is competitive with standard supervised fine-tuning while using significantly fewer training data and only requiring minimal feedback.

