 Overlap-free words were first studied by Axel Thue in two papers #ref5# #ref6# in 1906 and 1912. In particular he proved that there are infinite overlap-free binary words. In the following we consider binary words, and  so we say that a word is overlap-free if it does not have factors of the form $xx$ where $x$ is a nonempty word. Overlap-free words have been studied extensively (see \cite{arxivID-2006.10152-0} for a survey of results on overlap-free words and for \cite{arxivID-1705.04055-1} for a list of open problems on overlap-free words.

A word $w$ is \emph{square-free} if it does not have factors (blocks of consecutive letters) of the form $xx$ for nonempty $x$. A word $w$ is \emph{overlap-free} if it does not have factors of the form $xxyx$ for nonempty $x$ and $y$. The famous Thue's construction shows that there are infinite square-free words over a ternary alphabet. In 1906, Axel Thue also showed that there are infinite overlap-free binary words. In the following we consider binary words, and so we say that a word is overlap-free if it does not have factors of the form $xxyx$ for nonempty $x$ and $y$. Overlap-free words have been studied extensively (see \cite{arxivID-1801.05376-2} for a survey of results on overlap-free words and for \cite{arxivID-2207.09937-3} for a survey of results on abelian powers and abelian repetitions.

For a word $w$, let $|w|$ denote the length of $w$, and let $|w|_a$ denote the number of occurrences of the letter $a$ in $w$. For a word $w$, let $\overline{w}$ denote the word obtained by complementing the letters of $w$, i.e., replacing 0's by 1's and 1's by 0's. The word $w$ is \emph{unbordered} if it has no proper prefix that is also a suffix. The word $w$ is \emph{strongly unbordered} if it has no proper factor that is a nontrivial power. It is known that a word is unbordered if and only if it is strongly unbordered and its length is not a power of a prime number. A word $w$ is \emph{primitive} if it is not a proper power. A word $w$ is \emph{weakly primitive} if it is not a proper power and it is not a proper factor of a proper power. It is known that a word is primitive if and only if it is weakly primitive and its length is not a power of a prime number. For a word $w$, let $|w|_P$ denote the number of primitive factors of $w$, and let $|w|_{WP}$ denote the number of weakly primitive factors of $w$. A factor $x$ of $w$ is a \emph{middle factor} of $w$ if $w$ has factors $y$ and $z$ such that $w=xyz$. A factor $x$ of $w$ is a \emph{central factor} of $w$ if $w$ has factors $y$ and $z$ such that $w=xyyz$. A factor $x$ of $w$ is a \emph{central middle factor} of $w$ if $w$ has factors $y$ and $z$ such that $w=xyz=xyyz$.

The following theorem is the main result of the paper.

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:main}
Let $w$ be an overlap-free binary word. Then $|w|_{\text{middle}}\leq 4|w|_P+|w|_{WP}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{figure}
\begin{center}
\includegraphics{thefigure.pdf}
\caption{An overlap-free binary word $w$ and its factors. The bold factors are the central factors of $w$. The underlined factors are the central middle factors of $w$.}
\label{fig:thefigure}
\end{center}
\end{figure}

\begin{example}
Let $w=010110010110$. Then $|w|_P=5$ (the factors are $0101$, $0110$, $01$, $10$, and $1001$), $|w|_{WP}=2$ (the factors are $0101$ and $1001$), and $|w|_{\text{middle}}=7$ (the factors are $011$, $1001$, $0110$, $01$, $10$, $011001$, and $010110$).
\end{example}

Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is proved in Section~\ref{sec:proof}. In Section~\ref{sec:lower} we prove that there are infinitely many overlap-free binary words $w$ such that $|w|_{\text{middle}}=4|w|_P+|w|_{WP}$. <|section_end|>

