 We study the task of composed image retrieval, that is, finding an image from a large corpus that best matches a user query provided as an image-language pair. Unlike traditional content-based #ref38# or text-based #ref42# #ref24# image retrieval where a single modality is used to describe the target image, composed image retrieval involves both visual and textual modalities to specify the users intent. For humans the advantage of a bi-modal query is clear: some  visual details are hard to describe in words, while some textual details are hard to depict visually. For example, the query ``a man in a suit holding a briefcase'' is easier to describe in words than to draw. Likewise, the query ``a picture of a person standing in a field'' is easier to draw than to describe in words. Composed image retrieval has a wide range of applications, including product search \cite{arxivID-1609.07859-0}, image editing \cite{arxivID-2002.06484-1}, and image search engines \cite{arxivID-2401.10464-2}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.47\textwidth]{figs/teaser2.png}
\caption{An example of composed image retrieval. The image on the left is the input query, and the target image is on the right.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure}

To the best of our knowledge, existing work on composed image retrieval has only been applied to non-complex images within narrow domains, such as fashion products \cite{arxivID-1906.06620-3, arxivID-2003.12299-4, arxivID-2405.15451-5, arxivID-2210.15028-6}. This is because existing datasets for composed image retrieval are limited to narrow domains. For example, the Fashion-IQ \cite{arxivID-1905.12794-7} dataset consists of fashion products, and the Fashion-MNIST \cite{arxivID-2005.08170-8} dataset contains only simple fashion images. As a result, existing methods have only been able to reason about simple visual concepts such as color, shape, and texture, and are unable to tackle the more complex visual reasoning required for open-domain images. For example, the query ``a picture of a person standing in a field'' requires reasoning about the spatial relationship between the person and the field, which is difficult to capture with existing methods.

To address this issue, we collect the Composed Image Retrieval on Real-life images (CIRR) dataset, which consists of over 36,000 pairs of crowd-sourced, open-domain images with human-generated modifying text. The images were collected from the internet and the text was generated using a crowd-sourcing platform. The CIRR dataset is designed to be challenging, with a focus on complex visual reasoning. For example, the query ``a picture of a person standing in a field'' requires reasoning about the spatial relationship between the person and the field, which is difficult to capture with existing methods. We believe that CIRR will be a valuable resource for researchers working on composed image retrieval, and will help advance the state-of-the-art in this area.

To extend current methods to the open-domain, we propose CIRPLANT, a transformer based model that leverages rich pre-trained vision-and-language (V\&L) knowledge for modifying visual features conditioned on natural language. Retrieval is then done by nearest neighbor lookup on the modified features. CIRPLANT outperforms existing methods on open-domain images, while matching state-of-the-art accuracy on the existing narrow datasets, such as fashion. Together with the release of CIRR, we believe this work will inspire further research on composed image retrieval. Our dataset, code and pre-trained models are available at \url{https://cuberick-orion.github.io/CIRR/}.

Our contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We collect the Composed Image Retrieval on Real-life images (CIRR) dataset, which consists of over 36,000 pairs of crowd-sourced, open-domain images with human-generated modifying text.
\item We propose CIRPLANT, a transformer based model that leverages rich pre-trained vision-and-language (V\&L) knowledge for modifying visual features conditioned on natural language.
\item CIRPLANT outperforms existing methods on open-domain images, while matching state-of-the-art accuracy on the existing narrow datasets, such as fashion.
\end{itemize}
Related Work
\subsection{Vision-and-Language Pre-training}
Vision-and-Language (V\&L) pre-training has become a popular approach for learning joint representations of images and text, with applications in various tasks such as image captioning, visual question answering, and visual grounding. The key idea is to pre-train a model on a large-scale image-text dataset using self-supervised objectives, such as masked language modeling and image-text matching, and then fine-tune the model on downstream tasks. This approach has been shown to be effective in learning rich and transferable representations that can improve performance on a variety of tasks.

One of the earliest V\&L pre-training models is the VisualBERT model \cite{arxivID-1908.03557-9}, which uses a stack of Transformer layers to align text and image regions. The model is pre-trained on the Conceptual Captions dataset \cite{arxivID-2012.11691-10} using two visually-grounded language model objectives. VisualBERT is evaluated on four V\&L tasks, including visual question answering (VQA), visual commonsense reasoning (VCR), natural language for visual reasoning (NLVR2), and Flickr30K. The model outperforms or rivals with state-of-the-art models while being significantly simpler.

Another V\&L pre-training model is the ViLBERT model \cite{arxivID-1908.02265-11}, which extends the popular BERT architecture to a multi-modal two-stream model. The model processes both visual and textual inputs in separate streams that interact through co-attentional transformer layers. ViLBERT is pre-trained on the Conceptual Captions dataset using two proxy tasks, and is evaluated on four V\&L tasks, including VQA, VCR, referring expressions, and caption-based image retrieval. The model achieves state-of-the-art performance on all four tasks.

In addition to these two models, there are many other V\&L pre-training models proposed in the literature, such as LXMERT \cite{arxivID-1908.07490-12}, UNITER \cite{arxivID-1909.11740-13}, UNIMO \cite{arxivID-2012.15409-14}, Oscar \cite{arxivID-2004.06165-15}, and ViLT \cite{arxivID-2102.03334-16}

