\begin{itemize}
\item We introduce the CIRR dataset for composed image retrieval on real-life images.
\item We propose CIRPLANT, a transformer based model that leverages rich pre-trained vision-and-language knowledge for modifying visual features conditioned on natural language.
\item We demonstrate that CIRPLANT outperforms existing methods on open-domain images, while matching state-of-the-art accuracy on the existing narrow datasets, such as fashion.
\end{itemize}
Related Work
\textbf{Content-based Image Retrieval:} Content-based image retrieval (CBIR) \cite{arxivID-1706.06064-2} is a classical problem in computer vision, where the goal is to find images in a database that are similar to a query image. Early work on CBIR focused on low-level features, such as color, texture, and shape. However, these features are often not discriminative enough to capture the semantic content of an image. To address this issue, recent work has focused on using deep neural networks to extract high-level features that capture semantic information. For example, \cite{arxivID-1504.05133-3} used a pre-trained CNN to extract features from an image, and then used a nearest neighbor search to find similar images. 

\textbf{Text-based Image Retrieval:} Text-based image retrieval (TBIR) is the task of retrieving images from a database based on a text query. Early work on TBIR focused on using hand-crafted features, such as bag-of-words and Fisher vectors, to represent the text query and the images. However, these features are often not discriminative enough to capture the semantic content of the text and images. To address this issue, recent work has focused on using deep neural networks to extract features from the text and images. For example, \cite{arxivID-1406.5679-4} proposed a deep neural network that learns to embed both visual and language data into a common, multi-modal space. 

\textbf{Composed Image Retrieval:} Composed image retrieval is a challenging task that combines the challenges of CBIR and TBIR. The goal is to find images that are similar to a query that combines visual and textual information. Early work on composed image retrieval focused on using hand-crafted features, such as bag-of-words and Fisher vectors, to represent the query and the images. However, these features are often not discriminative enough to capture the semantic content of the query and images. To address this issue, recent work has focused on using deep neural networks to extract features from the query and images. For example, \cite{arxivID-1704.00763-5} proposed a deep neural network that combines visual and textual features to represent the query and images. 

\textbf{Vision-and-Language Pre-training:} Vision-and-language pre-training (V\&L) is a technique that uses large-scale datasets of images and text to learn representations that can be used for a variety of tasks, such as image captioning, visual question answering, and composed image retrieval. V\&L models are typically trained using self-supervised learning, where the model is trained to predict missing parts of the input data. For example, \cite{arxivID-2004.06165-6} proposed a V\&L model that uses a transformer-based architecture to learn cross-modal representations of images and text. <|section_end|>

