\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Self-supervised learning.} Self-supervised learning has recently emerged as a powerful technique for learning representations from unlabeled data. In contrast to supervised learning, where labels are scarce and expensive to obtain, self-supervised learning leverages the rich information present in unlabeled data to learn powerful representations. The key idea is to define a pretext task that can be solved using only unlabeled data, and to use the learned representation for a downstream task of interest. The pretext task should be designed to capture relevant information about the data, and to be solvable using only unlabeled data. For example, in the context of images, one can define the pretext task of predicting the orientation of an image \cite{arxivID-2202.04181-3}, the relative position of two patches \cite{arxivID-2206.00481-4}, or the color of an image \cite{arxivID-1603.08511-5}. These pretext tasks have been shown to learn representations that transfer well to a variety of downstream tasks, including image classification, object detection, and semantic segmentation. In the context of videos, one can define the pretext task of predicting the future frames of a video \cite{arxivID-1909.04656-6}, or the order in which the frames of a video are presented \cite{arxivID-1708.01246-7}

