 Modern computer vision systems represent images in high-dimensional embedding spaces. To unveil why an image is considered to be similar to a certain class, interest in understanding these embeddings, e.g., via conceptual explanations  #ref24# #ref27# #ref12# #ref48# #ref1# #ref22#, is increasing. The goal is to scrutinize a given encoder by decomposing  its embedding into interpretable components. For example, in a colorization task, the learned embedding of an image could be decomposed into a shape and a color component. Ideally, these components can be manipulated independently, thus disentangling them into axes of the embedding space.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{figs/teaser.pdf}
\caption{We discover components in an embedding space without distributional assumptions or training a generator. The components are disentangled into axes of the embedding space, i.e., they can be manipulated independently.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure}

Disentangling embedding components is a well-studied problem in machine learning. A common approach is to assume the components are independent and identically distributed (IID) \cite{arxivID-1404.2986-0, arxivID-2102.02876-1}. Then, the embedding components can be recovered by applying an ICA method to the embedding. However, such approaches are limited to linear embedding spaces and require the components to be IID, which is often violated in practice.

Another approach is to learn a generative model of the complex image-generating process and then apply a disentanglement method to the model's latent space. However, learning such a model requires large amounts of data and is computationally expensive. For example, the recent state-of-the-art method, DisenVAE \cite{arxivID-1812.02833-2}, trains a variational autoencoder (VAE) \cite{arxivID-1312.6114-3} with a carefully designed loss function. This process is time-consuming and the quality of the disentanglement depends on the complexity of the chosen image-generating process. As a result, DisenVAE has a limited ability to disentangle complex, high-dimensional embeddings such as those from a CLIP image encoder \cite{arxivID-2103.00020-4}.

In this work, we propose a novel approach to component discovery that addresses the aforementioned limitations. We assume that images are generated by a complex process consisting of multiple simpler functions, each responsible for generating a specific component of the image. For example, in a colorization task, there might be a function responsible for generating the shape of the object and another function responsible for generating the color. We assume that each function is independent and that the functions are applied in a specific order to generate the final image.

Based on these assumptions, we propose two novel methods for component discovery: Component Discovery by Maximum Likelihood (CDML) and Component Discovery by Maximum Marginal Likelihood (CDMML). These methods are based on the idea of maximizing the likelihood of the embedding given the component discovery functions. We show that, under certain assumptions, the component discovery functions can be learned by maximizing the likelihood of the embedding. We also provide theoretical guarantees for the identifiability of the components under these assumptions.

We evaluate our methods on several disentanglement tasks, including colorization, image inpainting, and image generation. We compare our methods to state-of-the-art disentanglement and component analysis models, including DisenVAE and ICA methods. Our results show that our methods achieve superior performance on all tasks and are more stable than other methods.

In summary, our contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose two novel methods for component discovery: CDML and CDMML. These methods are based on the idea of maximizing the likelihood of the embedding given the component discovery functions.
\item We provide theoretical guarantees for the identifiability of the components under certain assumptions.
\item We evaluate our methods on several disentanglement tasks and show that they achieve superior performance and are more stable than other methods.
\end{itemize}
Related Work
\paragraph{Disentanglement Learning.}
In recent years, there has been a growing interest in disentangling the latent variables of deep generative models. One approach is to use variational autoencoders (VAEs) \cite{arxivID-1312.6114-5}, which are generative models that learn a latent representation of the data. However, VAEs often fail to disentangle the latent variables, as the latent space is not designed to be interpretable. To address this issue, several disentanglement methods have been proposed, such as beta-VAE \cite{arxivID-1804.03599-6}, FactorVAE \cite{arxivID-1802.05983-7}, and DisenVAE \cite{arxivID-1812.02833-8}. These methods modify the VAE objective function to encourage the latent variables to be independent and interpretable.

Another approach is to use generative adversarial networks (GANs) \cite{arxivID-1406.2661-9}, which are generative models that learn a mapping from a simple distribution to a complex distribution. Several disentanglement methods have been proposed for GANs, such as InfoGAN \cite{arxivID-1606.03657-10} and DCGAN \cite{arxivID-1511.06434-11}. These methods modify the GAN objective function to encourage the latent variables to be independent and interpretable.

\paragraph{Independent Component Analysis.}
Independent component analysis (ICA) \cite{arxivID-1404.2986-12} is a statistical technique used to separate a set of mixed signals into their independent components. ICA assumes that the mixed signals are linear combinations of independent sources, and it aims to find a linear transformation that separates the sources. Several ICA methods have been proposed, such as FastICA \cite{arxivID-1505.07008-13} and JADE \cite{arxivID-1002.3332-14}. These methods are based on different assumptions about the sources, such as non-Gaussianity or sparsity.

\paragraph{Post-hoc Component Discovery.}
Post-hoc component discovery methods aim to identify interpretable components in the embedding space of a pre-trained encoder. These methods do not modify the encoder or the training process, but instead focus on analyzing the learned embedding space. Several post-hoc component discovery methods have been proposed, such as Concept Bottleneck \cite{arxivID-2007.04612-15}, Concept Whitening \cite{arxivID-2002.01650-16}, and Concept Factorization \cite{arxivID-2307.06913-17}

