The problem of component identification is also known as \emph{blind source separation} (BSS) or \emph{independent component analysis} (ICA) \cite{arxivID-1404.2986-1}. It has been studied extensively in the literature, but mostly in the context of finding statistically independent components.

We distinguish our work from existing approaches by two major characteristics. First, we do not rely on the assumption of statistical independence between components, which is often violated in practice. Second, we do not rely on generative models of the data distribution, which are difficult to train and often fail to capture the complexity of natural images.

\textbf{Our contributions.} We propose two novel component identification methods that do not rely on statistical independence of the components. They are based on the observation that components are composed by functions of the input variables. Thus, the components are correlated with the input variables and uncorrelated with each other. We show that the correlation between a component and the input variables is a sufficient statistic for identifying a component. We derive two novel component discovery methods and prove that they identify components under mild assumptions. Our first method, \emph{Corr2Comp}, relies on a simple correlation threshold. Our second method, \emph{Cov2Comp}, utilizes the covariance between two components. We show that the covariance between two components is a sufficient statistic for identifying components under mild assumptions. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our methods on a variety of synthetic and real-world datasets. Our methods outperform existing methods by a large margin and are able to identify components in a variety of settings. We also show that our methods are robust to violations of the assumptions and are able to identify components in a variety of settings. Our code is available at \href{https://github.com/alexander-petukhov/Disentangling-Visual-Embeddings-with-Minimal-Distributional-Assumptions}{https://github.com/alexander-petukhov/Disentangling-Visual-Embeddings-with-Minimal-Distributional-Assumptions}.
Related Work
\textbf{Component analysis.} Blind source separation \cite{arxivID-1404.2986-2, arxivID-1404.2986-3} and independent component analysis \cite{arxivID-1404.2986-4, arxivID-1404.2986-5} are well-studied problems in the signal processing literature. The goal is to recover the underlying components of a signal without knowing the mixing process. This problem is ill-posed and additional assumptions are needed to make the problem identifiable. One common assumption is that the components are statistically independent. This assumption is reasonable for natural signals, where the components are often uncorrelated. However, this assumption is violated in many computer vision tasks, where the components are often correlated. For example, in the case of the MNIST dataset, the components are the digits, which are correlated. Our work is the first to address this problem and propose a solution that does not rely on the assumption of statistical independence.

