We briefly review the AS decomposition. Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ be a bounded domain with Lipschitz boundary $\partial \Omega$. Let $\omega_1, \ldots, \omega_m \subset \Omega$ be open, bounded, connected Lipschitz domains, and let $\Omega_0 = \Omega \setminus \cup_{i=1}^m \omega_i$ be the background domain. The AS decomposition of the characteristic function $\chi_{\Omega}$ is a sequence of characteristic functions $\chi_{\Omega_0}, \chi_{\omega_1}, \ldots, \chi_{\omega_m}$ that are mutually orthogonal in the sense of the $L^2(\Omega)$ inner product. The AS decomposition is associated with a piecewise constant function $u$ that is harmonic in $\Omega_0$ and in each $\omega_i$ and satisfies $u = 0$ on $\partial \Omega$. Then, $u$ is a solution of the following eigenvalue problem:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ASeigenvalueproblem}
\left\{
\begin{array}{rcll}
-\Delta u & = & \lambda u & \mbox{in $\Omega$}, \\
u & = & 0 & \mbox{on $\partial \Omega$}.
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
