 The online tracking ecosystem employs increasingly sophisticated tracking techniques to track users across the web  #ref56# #ref25# #ref70# #ref112#. In addition to well-known stateful tracking using third-party cookies, trackers have now started to use more intrusive stateless tracking techniques such as browser fingerprinting to gather device-specific identifying information captured through various HTTP header fields and APIs  #ref27# #ref78# #ref71# #ref72# #ref16# #ref25# #ref54#. Stateless tracking is more intrusive than stateful tracking because the  device-specific information is collected without the user's consent. Moreover, unlike cookies, stateless tracking is harder to detect and mitigate. A browser fingerprint is a unique identifier created by combining the information exposed by multiple different web APIs to identify a user across the web. It has been shown that the combination of multiple APIs can create a unique fingerprint for 99.98\% of users \cite{arxivID-1109.2282-0}.  In 2016, the W3C's Web Identity Working Group proposed a standard for the user's device identifier that could be used for browser fingerprinting. The group has since been disbanded, and the proposed standard is no longer under active development. Nevertheless, browser fingerprinting is still a widespread tracking technique used by many websites \cite{arxivID-1905.09581-1}.

Existing approaches for detecting browser fingerprinting are limited to detecting specific fingerprinting techniques at a particular point in time. Thus, they are unable to systematically detect novel fingerprinting techniques that abuse different web APIs. In this paper, we propose \toolname, a machine learning approach that leverages longitudinal measurements of web API usage on top-100K websites over the last decade, for early detection of new and evolving browser fingerprinting techniques. We show that \toolname is able to early detect the abuse of newly introduced properties of already known (e.g., WebGL, Sensor) and as well as previously unknown (e.g., Gamepad, Clipboard) APIs for browser fingerprinting. To the best of our knowledge, \toolname is also first to detect the abuse of the Visibility API for ephemeral fingerprinting in the wild. \toolname is able to detect the abuse of 15 APIs for fingerprinting, including 8 APIs that have never been used for fingerprinting before. We also find that the popularity of fingerprinting APIs has been steadily increasing over the last decade. Moreover, we show that the prevalence of fingerprinting APIs is higher in the case of third-party than first-party scripts. \toolname is able to detect the abuse of 15 APIs for fingerprinting, including 8 APIs that have never been used for fingerprinting before.

