The use of PET/CT imaging has significantly improved the management of cancer patients, however, this resource intensive exam is particularly expensive and time consuming. PET/CT exams require patients to undergo intravenous injection of a radioactive tracer (usually fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) or Fluoromisonidazole (FMISO)) followed by a 60-90 minute exam. The exam is then interpreted by a nuclear medicine physician and a radiologist. In addition to the time required to perform and interpret the exam, PET/CT imaging requires a highly specialized infrastructure and is typically performed at a tertiary cancer center. The cost of a PET/CT exam in the United States is approximately \$3,000, making this exam inaccessible to many patients.

The development of machine learning algorithms to automate the interpretation of PET/CT scans has the potential to significantly improve the efficiency and accessibility of cancer management. However, there are several challenges that must be addressed in order to develop robust and reliable algorithms. First, there is a lack of available longitudinal datasets that include detailed annotations of treatment response. Second, PET/CT exams are complex, large multimodal imaging examinations that require specialized expertise to interpret. Third, traditional supervised machine learning approaches require large amounts of labeled data, which can be difficult to obtain for this type of imaging data.

In this paper, we propose a novel machine learning algorithm for automated assessment of treatment response from PET/CT exams using weak supervision. We leverage the standard uptake values (SUVmax) in associated radiology reports to train our model to predict treatment response. We evaluate our algorithm on a large dataset of 1,954 paired PET/CT exams and demonstrate that it achieves high accuracy and agreement with clinical scoring systems. Our approach has the potential to provide clinicians with a valuable tool to rapidly and consistently interpret change over time in longitudinal multi-modal imaging exams. <|section_end|>

 Visual Place Recognition (VPR) is a critical component for robotic and autonomous platforms: providing the ability to recognise previously visited locations solely using image data, which is beneficial for a number of downstream tasks including Simultaneous Localization And Mapping (SLAM) and navigation  #ref1# #ref2# #ref3# #ref4#. VPR is challenging due to the range of appearance and viewpoint changes that can arise when navigating. Appearance changes include diverse seasonal changes, such as summer to  winter, as well as lighting changes (day to night) and weather changes (sunny to rainy). Viewpoint changes include variations in position (e.g., from a moving vehicle to a walking pedestrian) and orientation (e.g., from a ground-based robot to a flying drone). These challenges make it difficult for a single VPR technique to achieve consistently high performance across all variations, which has led to the development of many different VPR techniques, each with their own strengths and weaknesses. One recent promising approach to the VPR problem has been to fuse the place recognition estimates of multiple complementary VPR techniques using methods such as SRAL \cite{arxivID-2108.08443-0} and multi-process fusion \cite{arxivID-1903.03305-1}. These approaches come with a substantial practical limitation: they require all potential VPR methods to be brute-force run before they are selectively fused. The obvious solution to this limitation is to predict the viable subset of methods ahead of time, but this is challenging because it requires a predictive signal within the imagery itself that is indicative of high performance methods. Here we propose an alternative approach that instead starts with a known single base VPR technique, and learns to predict the most complementary additional VPR technique to fuse with it, that results in the largest improvement in performance. The key innovation here is to use a dimensionally reduced difference vector between the query image and the top-retrieved reference image using this baseline technique as the predictive signal of the most complementary additional technique, both during training and inference. We demonstrate that our approach can train a single network to select performant, complementary technique pairs across datasets which span multiple modes of transportation (train, car, walking) as well as to generalise to unseen datasets, outperforming multiple baseline strategies for manually selecting the best technique pairs based on the same training data.

