\subsection{Semantic Segmentation}
Semantic segmentation is a fundamental task in computer vision that involves assigning a class label to each pixel in an image. The goal of semantic segmentation is to provide a pixel-level understanding of the image, which can be useful for a wide range of applications such as autonomous driving, medical imaging, and robotics. With the advent of deep learning, convolutional neural networks (CNNs) have become the de-facto standard for semantic segmentation. Several approaches have been proposed to improve the performance of CNNs for semantic segmentation, including fully convolutional networks (FCNs) \cite{arxivID-1605.06211-2}, encoder-decoder architectures \cite{arxivID-1505.07293-3}, and attention mechanisms \cite{arxivID-2309.10217-4}. Recently, transformer-based models have also been proposed for semantic segmentation, achieving state-of-the-art performance on several benchmark datasets \cite{arxivID-2207.09339-5, arxivID-2207.04044-6}.

\subsection{Unsupervised Domain Adaptation}
Unsupervised domain adaptation is a machine learning technique that aims to adapt a model trained on a labeled source domain to an unlabeled target domain. The goal of unsupervised domain adaptation is to minimize the distribution shift between the source and target domains, which can be achieved by aligning the feature distributions or by learning domain-invariant representations. Unsupervised domain adaptation has been successfully applied to a wide range of computer vision tasks, including image classification \cite{arxivID-1301.3224-7}, object detection \cite{arxivID-2011.07205-8}, and semantic segmentation \cite{arxivID-2107.11052-9}.

\subsection{Self-Training}
Self-training is a semi-supervised learning technique that involves iteratively training a model on labeled data and using the model's predictions on unlabeled data to generate additional training examples. Self-training has been successfully applied to a wide range of computer vision tasks, including image classification \cite{arxivID-2109.00778-10}, object detection \cite{arxivID-2007.09162-11}, and semantic segmentation \cite{arxivID-2103.17105-12}.

