A community is a group of nodes with dense internal connections and sparse external ones \cite{arxivID-0906.0612-0}. The main challenge of community detection is to find the communities that are more tightly connected than the rest of the network. Since the community structure is not well defined, there are many definitions of community \cite{arxivID-0906.0612-1}. The most commonly used one is the modularity metric \cite{arxivID-0906.0612-2}, which is defined as:
\begin{equation}
Q = \frac{1}{2m}\sum_{i,j}\left(A_{ij} - \frac{k_ik_j}{2m}\right)\delta(c_i, c_j)
\end{equation}
where $A_{ij}$ is the adjacency matrix, $m$ is the total number of edges, $k_i$ is the degree of node $i$, $c_i$ is the community of node $i$ and $\delta(c_i, c_j)$ is the Kronecker delta function.

