A large body of CL methods have been developed to address the stability-plasticity dilemma. The existing CL methods can be roughly categorized into three groups: replay-based methods, regularization-based methods, and architecture-based methods. The replay-based methods store a part of the past data to the episodic memory and replay them to the model when learning new information. The regularization-based methods use the regularization term to prevent catastrophic interference and to maintain the past knowledge. The architecture-based methods use dynamic architectures to learn new information without forgetting past knowledge.

However, the existing CL methods are developed for the task setup with clean labels. In real-world applications, however, the labels of the data stream can be contaminated with incorrect labels due to noisy user behaviors, and the labels of the data stream can be blurry due to the blurred task boundaries. Thus, the existing CL methods can suffer from the noise in the data stream, and it is desirable to develop a new CL method that can learn under the noisy data stream.

In this paper, we propose a novel task setup of continual learning with blurry data stream and noisy labels, and develop a new CL method to address the task. We argue the importance of both diversity and purity of the episodic memory of the CL model. To balance diversity and purity in the episodic memory, we propose a novel strategy to manage and use the memory by a unified approach of label noise aware diverse sampling and robust learning with semi-supervised learning. Our empirical validations on four real-world or synthetic noise datasets (CIFAR10 and 100, mini-WebVision, and Food-101N) exhibit that our method significantly outperforms prior arts in this realistic and challenging continual learning scenario.
