 Sequential labeling tasks are ubiquitous among NLP applications. Tasks ranging from syntactic analysis (e.g., POS tagging and phrase chunking) to semantic analysis (e.g., named entity recognition, slot filling, and query segmentation), are critical components in end-to-end applications, such as search engines and goal-oriented dialog systems. Advances in pretraining of generative language models (LMs) like T5  #ref14# and mT5  #ref25# have enabled us to use the same training  framework for all these tasks. This framework is to train a Seq2Seq model to maximize the likelihood of the gold labeled sequence for each input. This objective, while simple and effective, is not well aligned with the desiderata of many practical applications.

Consider for example, the task of slot filling in a goal-oriented dialog system. The goal here is to assign a slot label to each token in the input utterance. This is a sequence labeling problem. But a system that is deployed in the wild will often need to make multiple predictions, and then choose the best one. For instance, the system may have to choose the top-k predictions, where k is a system parameter that can be tuned to control the trade-off between the precision and recall of the predictions. This kind of multi-prediction scenario is common in real-world systems, but the traditional supervised learning framework does not take it into account. It only optimizes for the likelihood of the gold labeled sequence, and thus may produce suboptimal results when evaluated on the top-k predictions. Thus, a better approach is to train the model directly on the desired evaluation metric. For example, we can train it to maximize the likelihood of the top-k predictions, or the precision-recall trade-off of the top-k predictions.

In this paper, we propose a simple yet effective framework for Generative Reward Optimization Of Text sequences, called \algname. \algname{} works by training a generative sequential labeling model to match the decoder output distribution with that of the (black-box) reward function. Using an iterative training regime, we first generate prediction candidates, then correct errors in them, and finally contrast those candidates (based on their reward values). As demonstrated via extensive experiments on four public benchmarks, \algname{} significantly improves all reward metrics. Furthermore, \algname{} also leads to improvements of the overall decoder distribution as evidenced by the quality gains of the top-$k$ candidates.

The main contributions of this paper are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item We propose a simple yet effective framework for Generative Reward Optimization Of Text sequences, called \algname{}.
\item We demonstrate the effectiveness of \algname{} on four public benchmarks, showing significant improvements on all reward metrics.
\item We also show that \algname{} leads to improvements of the overall decoder distribution as evidenced by the quality gains of the top-$k$ candidates.
\end{enumerate}

The rest of the paper is organized as follows. In Section 2, we provide background on sequential labeling and reward optimization. In Section 3, we describe the proposed \algname{} framework. In Section 4, we present experimental results on four public benchmarks. In Section 5, we discuss related work. Finally, in Section 6, we conclude with some future directions.
Related Work
\subsection{Sequential Labeling}
\label{sec:seq-labeling}
Sequential labeling is a fundamental NLP task that involves assigning a label to each token in a given input sequence. This is a challenging task because the labels are not independent of each other, and they have to be predicted jointly. In this paper, we consider a standard supervised learning setup for sequential labeling. We have a training dataset of input sequences and their corresponding gold labeled sequences. We use this dataset to train a Seq2Seq model to predict the labels for the input sequences. The model is trained to maximize the likelihood of the gold labeled sequences. 

\subsection{Reward Optimization}
\label{sec:reward-optimization}
Reward optimization is a technique used to train a model to optimize a given reward function. The reward function is a black-box function that takes the output of the model as input and returns a reward value. The goal of reward optimization is to train the model to generate outputs that maximize the reward value. This is done by using a contrastive loss function that encourages the model to generate outputs with high reward values and discourage outputs with low reward values. <|section_end|>

