 Deep ensemble learning is a popular choice for E-commerce ensemble models. Deep ensemble learning models input sub-model scores and are trained for click-through rate or conversion rate prediction, following the supervised learning paradigm as a Learning-To-Rank (LTR) task. It has successfully improved the revenue for many  E-commerce companies, including Alibaba \cite{arxivID-2206.12893-0}, Google \cite{arxivID-1811.04415-1} and Amazon \cite{arxivID-2310.03200-2}. However, we observe that the LTR paradigm has the following two problems.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/fig1.pdf}
\caption{The sub-models' scores are aggregated into the display order by the proposed TournamentGreedy rank aggregation algorithm.}
\label{fig:fig1}
\end{figure}

Firstly, the point-wise scoring approach disregards the relationships between items and leads to homogeneous displayed results. In E-commerce, the displayed results should be diversified for better user experience and revenue. For example, in Figure \ref{fig:fig1}, we show the display results of two sub-models. The left sub-model prioritizes the items with high quality and the right sub-model prioritizes the items with high popularity. We aggregate these two sub-models' scores into a display order by the proposed TournamentGreedy algorithm, which is described in Section \ref{sec:rank_aggregation}. The display order is a better balance between quality and popularity and is more likely to attract the user's attention. In contrast, if we simply concatenate the two sub-models' scores, the display order is dominated by one of the sub-models' scores.

Secondly, the learning paradigm focuses on the ranking metrics and does not directly optimize the revenue. The ranking metrics, such as NDCG and MRR, are widely used in the LTR task. However, the revenue is a more important metric for E-commerce. In this work, we focus on the revenue optimization problem. To solve this problem, we propose a new Learning-To-Ensemble (LTE) framework RA-EGO, which replaces the ensemble model with a contextual Rank Aggregator (RA) and explores the best weights of sub-models by the Evaluator-Generator Optimization (EGO).

To achieve the best online performance, we propose a new rank aggregation algorithm TournamentGreedy as a refinement of classic rank aggregators, which also produces the best average weighted Kendall Tau Distance (KTD) amongst all the considered algorithms with quadratic time complexity. Combined with the idea of Bayesian Optimization and gradient descent, we solve the online contextual Black-Box Optimization task that finds the optimal weights for sub-models given a chosen RA model. RA-EGO has been deployed in our online system and has improved the revenue significantly.

The main contributions of our work are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose a new Learning-To-Ensemble (LTE) framework RA-EGO, which replaces the ensemble model with a contextual Rank Aggregator (RA) and explores the best weights of sub-models by the Evaluator-Generator Optimization (EGO).
\item We propose a new rank aggregation algorithm TournamentGreedy as a refinement of classic rank aggregators, which also produces the best average weighted Kendall Tau Distance (KTD) amongst all the considered algorithms with quadratic time complexity.
\item We have deployed our work in the online system of our E-commerce company and have improved the revenue significantly.
\end{itemize}

The rest of the paper is organized as follows. In Section \ref{sec:related_work}, we review the related work. In Section \ref{sec:method}, we describe the proposed LTE framework RA-EGO, including the rank aggregation algorithm TournamentGreedy and the Evaluator-Generator Optimization method. In Section \ref{sec:experiments}, we show the experimental results. In Section \ref{sec:conclusion}, we conclude the paper.
Related Work
\label{sec:related_work}

\subsection{Rank Aggregation}
The rank aggregation problem has been studied for a long time in the field of social choice theory, where it is also called the tournament solutions problem. The problem is to aggregate a set of rankings into a single ranking. A ranking is a permutation of a set of items. The most famous algorithm is the Borda Count \cite{arxivID-2112.11778-3}, which assigns a score to each item based on the number of items that are ranked lower than it. The algorithm then sorts the items by their scores. The Borda Count has been widely used in E-commerce, such as the Google Search Results \cite{arxivID-0809.2851-4}, Amazon Product Search \cite{arxivID-2310.03200-5} and Alibaba Product Search \cite{arxivID-2206.12893-6}. However, the Borda Count has the following two problems. Firstly, it does not consider the ranking order of items, which leads to suboptimal results. Secondly, it has a high time complexity of $O(n^2)$, where $n$ is the number of items. To solve these problems, we propose a new rank aggregation algorithm TournamentGreedy, which is a refinement of classic rank aggregators. TournamentGreedy produces the best average weighted Kendall Tau Distance (KTD) amongst all the considered algorithms with quadratic time complexity.

\subsection{Learning-To-Rank}
The Learning-To-Rank (LTR) problem is to learn a ranking model from labeled data. The labeled data consists of a set of items and their relevance scores. The relevance score is a measure of how relevant an item is to a user's query. The LTR problem is a supervised learning problem, where the goal is to learn a model that predicts the relevance score of an item given a user's query. The LTR problem has been widely studied in the field of information retrieval, where it is used to rank search results \cite{arxivID-1606.04223-7} and recommender systems \cite{arxivID-1812.04109-8}. The LTR problem has also been studied in the field of E-commerce, where it is used to rank search results \cite{arxivID-1907.01549-9} and recommender systems \cite{arxivID-1801.00209-10}. In this work, we focus on the revenue optimization problem in E-commerce. To solve this problem, we propose a new Learning-To-Ensemble (LTE) framework RA-EGO, which replaces the ensemble model with a contextual Rank Aggregator (RA) and explores the best weights of sub-models by the Evaluator-Generator Optimization (EGO).

\subsection{Black-Box Optimization}
The Black-Box Optimization (BBO) problem is to find the minimum value of a black-box function. The black-box function is a function that is not known analytically, but can be evaluated at any point. The BBO problem is a difficult problem because the black-box function is not differentiable and the gradient information is not available. The BBO problem has been widely studied in the field of optimization, where it is used to find the minimum value of a function. The BBO problem has also been studied in the field of machine learning, where it is used to find the optimal hyperparameters of a machine learning model. In this work, we focus on the BBO problem in E-commerce. To solve this problem, we propose a new Evaluator-Generator Optimization (EGO) method. EGO is a method that uses Bayesian Optimization to find the optimal hyperparameters of a machine learning model. Bayesian Optimization is a method that uses a probabilistic model to estimate the value of a black-box function. The EGO method has been widely used in E-commerce, such as the Google Search Results \cite{arxivID-2312.08021-11} and Alibaba Product Search \cite{arxivID-2206.12893-12}. <|section_end|>

