 Empowering machines with commonsense has become a hot topic recently. Past research efforts for this problem include the construction of various data sets and models. Several commonsense data sets have been commonly used in past work to  evaluate machines' commonsense capability, such as Winograd Schema Challenge (WSC) \cite{arxivID-1411.4109-0}, CommonsenseQA \cite{arxivID-1811.00937-1}, and SocialIQA \cite{arxivID-1904.09728-2}. While the data sets are different, they all aim to evaluate machines' ability to perform commonsense reasoning. There has been a lot of work on this problem. The most successful methods are based on pretrained language models (PLMs), such as BERT \cite{arxivID-1810.04805-3}, RoBERTa \cite{arxivID-1907.11692-4}, and XLNet \cite{arxivID-1906.08237-5}. They have achieved remarkable success on many NLP tasks. However, recent work \cite{arxivID-2209.00599-6} has shown that PLMs have not fully learned commonsense knowledge and are still vulnerable to semantic variations that require commonsense knowledge. This is because the training data of PLMs is not sufficient for learning all commonsense knowledge.

To improve the commonsense capability of PLMs, some recent work \cite{arxivID-1910.07713-7, arxivID-1909.02151-8} has focused on incorporating external knowledge bases, such as ConceptNet \cite{arxivID-1612.03975-9} and ATOMIC \cite{arxivID-1811.00146-10}. They have shown that incorporating such knowledge bases can improve PLMs' performance on commonsense tasks. However, their models are complicated and require additional training on the knowledge bases. This makes the models hard to train and less flexible for different tasks. Thus, there is still room for improvement on this problem.

In this paper, we propose two simple approaches to infuse external knowledge bases into PLMs. The first approach is to generate token embeddings using the knowledge bases and add them to the PLMs' token embeddings. The second approach is to generate sentence embeddings using the knowledge bases and concatenate them with the PLMs' output. We show that both approaches can improve PLMs' performance on SocialIQA, a social commonsense task, in both limited and full training data regimes.
Related Work
\subsection{External Commonsense Knowledge Graphs}
External commonsense knowledge graphs, such as ConceptNet \cite{arxivID-1612.03975-11} and ATOMIC \cite{arxivID-1811.00146-12}, provide rich information about words and their relationships. In this work, we use ConceptNet. It consists of concepts (words) and their relationships. There are 12 types of relationships, such as \textit{Synonym} and \textit{AtLocation}. Figure \ref{fig:conceptnet} shows an example of ConceptNet. It contains three concepts, \textit{piano}, \textit{musical instrument}, and \textit{keyboard}. \textit{piano} is a \textit{Synonym} of \textit{musical instrument} and \textit{piano} is \textit{AtLocation} of \textit{keyboard}. 

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{conceptnet.pdf}
\caption{An example of ConceptNet.}
\label{fig:conceptnet}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Pretrained Language Models}
PLMs, such as BERT \cite{arxivID-1810.04805-13}, RoBERTa \cite{arxivID-1907.11692-14}, and XLNet \cite{arxivID-1906.08237-15}, have achieved remarkable success on many NLP tasks. They are usually pretrained on large text corpora with the objective of predicting masked words or reconstructing the original input. After pretraining, the models are fine-tuned for different downstream tasks. In this work, we use XLNet as the PLM. <|section_end|>

