 Self-driving cars (SDCs) are autonomous systems that collect, analyze, and leverage sensor data from the surrounding environment to control physical actuators at run-time  #ref14# #ref4#. Testing automation for SDCs is vital to ensure their safety and reliability  #ref50# #ref51#, but it presents several limitations and drawbacks:   (i) the limited ability to repeat tests under the same conditions due to ever-changing environmental factors #ref51#;  (ii) the difficulty to test the systems in safety-critical scenarios (to avoid irreversible damages caused by dreadful outcomes) #ref44# #ref81# #ref48#;  (iii) not being able to guarantee the systems reliability in its operational design domain due to a lack of testing under a wide range of execution conditions #ref50#. The usage of virtual simulation environments addresses several of the challenges  mentioned above. It allows testing SDCs in a safe and repeatable way, under different environmental conditions, and under a wide range of execution conditions. This approach can be used to test SDCs before they are deployed and executed in the real world. However, virtual simulation-based tests are expensive to run, and they are too many to be run frequently within limited time constraints \cite{arxivID-2211.11409-0}. In this context, test case prioritization (TCP) techniques are useful to increase the ability to detect faults earlier with the same level of test execution cost \cite{arxivID-1806.09774-1}. TCP techniques reorder test cases according to a pre-defined goal (e.g., maximizing the ability to detect faults). In this paper, we investigate TCP techniques to prioritize simulation-based tests for SDCs. Our goal is to prioritize tests that can detect faults with the least amount of execution time. To achieve this goal, we introduce a new framework called SDC-Prioritizer that uses two evolutionary approaches to prioritize the test cases according to static features of the roads we designed to be used within the driving scenarios. These features can be collected without running the tests, which means that they do not require past execution results. The first approach, called SO-SDC-Prioritizer, uses a single-objective genetic algorithm (SO-GA) to find test cases that cover the most diverse features of the roads in the driving scenarios. The second approach, called MO-SDC-Prioritizer, uses a multi-objective genetic algorithm (MO-GA) to find a trade-off between executing the less expensive tests and the most diverse test cases earlier. We evaluated our approaches on a dataset of 10'320 simulation-based tests generated with the CARLA simulator \cite{arxivID-1711.03938-2}. Our results show that  MO-SDC-Prioritizer significantly (p-value$\leq$0.05) improves the ability to detect safety-critical failures at the same level of execution time compared to random and greedy-based test case orderings. Besides, our study indicates that multi-objective meta-heuristics outperform single-objective approaches when prioritizing simulation-based tests for SDCs. MO-SDC-Prioritizer prioritizes test cases with a large improvement in fault detection while its overhead (up to 0.45\% of the test execution cost) is negligible.

\textbf{Paper structure.} Section \ref{sec:background} introduces the background and related work. Section \ref{sec:approach} presents our approach. Section \ref{sec:evaluation} describes the experimental setup and results. Section \ref{sec:threats} discusses the threats to validity. Section \ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper.
Related Work
\label{sec:background}

\subsection{Self-driving cars}
Self-driving cars (SDCs) are autonomous systems that collect, analyze, and leverage sensor data from the surrounding environment to control physical actuators at run-time. These systems use a combination of sensors (e.g., cameras, lidar, radar) to perceive the environment, and machine learning algorithms to analyze the data and make decisions. SDCs are designed to operate without human intervention and can perform tasks such as lane keeping, obstacle avoidance, and adaptive cruise control. Testing SDCs is challenging because they operate in complex and dynamic environments and have a high potential for catastrophic failures. Therefore, testing SDCs requires a combination of testing techniques, including simulation-based testing, in-field testing, and human-in-the-loop testing.

\subsection{Simulation-based testing for SDCs}
Simulation-based testing is a technique used to test the performance and reliability of SDCs in a safe and controlled environment. It involves creating a virtual environment that mimics real-world conditions, such as weather, traffic, and road conditions, and then testing the SDC in that environment. Simulation-based testing is particularly useful for testing SDCs because it allows testers to test the system in a safe and controlled environment, which is not always possible in the real world. For instance, testing an SDC in a virtual environment can help identify potential safety hazards, such as collisions with pedestrians or other vehicles, and allow testers to refine the system's design and control algorithms. However, simulation-based testing is not without its limitations. For instance, it can be challenging to accurately model the complex dynamics of real-world driving conditions, which can lead to discrepancies between the virtual and real-world environments. Additionally, simulation-based testing can be computationally expensive, which can limit the number of tests that can be run in a given time frame.

\subsection{Test case prioritization for SDCs}
Test case prioritization (TCP) is a technique used to improve the efficiency of software testing by prioritizing test cases based on their potential to detect faults. TCP techniques have been extensively studied in the software engineering literature, and several techniques have been proposed, including dynamic and static TCP techniques \cite{arxivID-1801.05917-3}. Dynamic TCP techniques rely on run-time execution information, such as coverage data, to prioritize test cases. In contrast, static TCP techniques operate directly on source and test code and do not require run-time execution information. Static TCP techniques have the advantage of being able to prioritize test cases without running the tests, which can be particularly useful when testing SDCs. However, static TCP techniques may not be as effective as dynamic TCP techniques in detecting faults in complex systems like SDCs. <|section_end|>

