 Knowledge graphs (KGs) have been the backbone of many knowledge-driven AI applications  #ref57# #ref53# #ref33# #ref23#, where factual knowledge about the real world are described as graphs of entities (nodes) and relations (edges). Yet, facts in real-world are varying over time instead of being persistently unchanged. For example, #eq0# has been true only for  a short period of time. Therefore, temporal knowledge graphs (TKGs) \cite{arxivID-1103.5046-0} are proposed to model the temporal evolution of the facts in KGs, which are sequences of knowledge graphs based on time. For example, in Figure \ref{fig:fig1}, $KG_{t1}$ and $KG_{t2}$ are two knowledge graphs at timestamps $t1$ and $t2$, respectively. The TKG is then a sequence of such knowledge graphs. The facts of the TKG are changing over time, where the relations of $KG_{t2}$ are the same as those of $KG_{t1}$ except for the relation of the birthplace of \textit{Albert Einstein} which is changed from \textit{Ulm} to \textit{Praha} at timestamp $t2$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{./figs/fig1.pdf}
\caption{An example of temporal knowledge graph (TKG) with two knowledge graphs at two timestamps. The relations of $KG_{t2}$ are the same as those of $KG_{t1}$ except for the relation of the birthplace of \textit{Albert Einstein} which is changed from \textit{Ulm} to \textit{Praha} at timestamp $t2$.}
\label{fig:fig1}
\end{figure}

Temporal knowledge graph completion (TKGC) aims to predict the missing facts in TKGs, which is a fundamental problem in many applications, such as recommendation systems \cite{arxivID-2101.03054-1} and question answering \cite{arxivID-2106.09013-2}. However, the TKGs are highly incomplete \cite{arxivID-2305.05403-3}, which poses challenges for the TKGC. To tackle this challenge, previous research \cite{arxivID-2201.08236-4} suggests diverse approaches to TKGC that use historical information, such as using the historical information as the input of the models \cite{arxivID-2012.08492-5}, or using the historical information to initialize the embeddings of the models \cite{arxivID-2009.14653-6}. However, less attention has been given to the hierarchies within the historical information at different timestamps, which are the chronological hierarchies between the knowledge graphs at different timestamps and the hierarchical levels of knowledge graphs at the same timestamp.

The chronological hierarchies between the knowledge graphs at different timestamps are derived from the chronology in the sequence of the TKG. The hierarchical level of a knowledge graph is determined by the number of its relations. Intuitively, a knowledge graph with more relations has higher hierarchical level than another knowledge graph with fewer relations. For example, $KG_{t2}$ has a higher hierarchical level than $KG_{t1}$, as $KG_{t2}$ has a relation \textit{place of birth} which is not included in $KG_{t1}$. In this paper, we use the term \textit{hierarchical level} to refer to the number of relations in a knowledge graph. In general, the knowledge graphs at the later timestamps have more relations than the knowledge graphs at the earlier timestamps. Therefore, the chronological hierarchies between the knowledge graphs at different timestamps are represented by embedding the knowledge graphs as vectors in a common hyperbolic space. Furthermore, diverse hierarchical levels of knowledge graphs are represented by adjusting the curvatures of hyperbolic embeddings of their entities and relations. We propose HyperVC, which utilizes hyperbolic space that better encodes the hierarchies than Euclidean space. The hyperbolic embeddings of the entities and relations of a knowledge graph are determined by the curvatures of the hyperbolic space. The curvatures are adjusted by the hierarchical levels of the knowledge graphs. For example, the curvatures of the hyperbolic embeddings of the entities and relations of $KG_{t2}$ are lower than those of $KG_{t1}$, which represents that $KG_{t2}$ has a higher hierarchical level than $KG_{t1}$.

The contributions of this paper are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose HyperVC, which utilizes hyperbolic space that better encodes the hierarchies than Euclidean space.
\item The chronological hierarchies between the knowledge graphs at different timestamps are represented by embedding the knowledge graphs as vectors in a common hyperbolic space.
\item Diverse hierarchical levels of knowledge graphs are represented by adjusting the curvatures of hyperbolic embeddings of their entities and relations.
\item Experiments on four benchmark datasets show substantial improvements, especially on the datasets with higher hierarchical levels.
\end{itemize} <|section_end|>

