 Contextual speech recognition aims at addressing the long-tail word problem in end-to-end ASR systems by incorporating contextual knowledge, and has become increasingly important in many applications  #ref1# #ref2# #ref3# #ref4# #ref5# #ref6# #ref7# #ref8# #ref9# #ref10# #ref11# #ref12# #ref13# #ref14# #ref15# #ref16#. Informative contextual knowledge can be extracted from text-based resources such as a users contact book, playlist and the ontology in a dialogue system, or from other modalities such as presentation slides or objects in a scene. Such contextual knowledge is often integrated via contextual biasing which represents contextual knowledge as a biasing list  of words, and is incorporated into the decoding process.

A common approach for contextual biasing is to use a language model (LM) that is biased towards the biasing words \cite{arxivID-1806.10215-0, arxivID-2106.01451-1, arxivID-2201.11867-2, arxivID-1907.01677-3, arxivID-1210.8440-4, arxivID-1911.10235-5, arxivID-2207.04176-6}. However, this approach requires an external LM and a separate decoding pass, and is thus computationally inefficient, especially for large biasing lists. Another approach is to use shallow fusion \cite{arxivID-cmp-lg/9603001-7, arxivID-cmp-lg/9707006-8, arxivID-cmp-lg/9608018-9, arxivID-1301.2318-10, arxivID-1802.00254-11, arxivID-2004.00967-12}, which fuses an LM with the acoustic model (AM) during decoding, to incorporate contextual knowledge in the AM. However, this approach requires the biasing words to be known in advance, and is thus less flexible.

More recently, end-to-end contextual ASR systems have been developed. The pointer generator (PGen) component proposed in \cite{arxivID-1603.08148-13, arxivID-1603.08148-14, arxivID-1901.11492-15, arxivID-1905.01975-16} was adapted to end-to-end ASR systems \cite{arxivID-2012.02012-17, arxivID-2207.03169-18, arxivID-2309.07369-19, arxivID-1902.07178-20, arxivID-1912.03363-21, arxivID-2302.08579-22}

 The cloud computing paradigm is a service provisioning model that provides user access to scalable computation, networking, and storage resources in the cloud data centers  #ref1#. Cloud service providers provide flexibility and efficiency for end-users by providing services such as software as service (SaaS), platform as a service (PaaS), and infrastructure as a service (IaaS)  #ref2#. Cloud computing has tremendously changed how we live, work, and study . However, as Internet-of-Things (IoT) applications become more prevalent, cloud computing faces new challenges in providing services, including high latency, low reliability, and limited scalability \cite{arxivID-1803.02890-0}. For example, IoT applications often generate large amounts of data that need to be processed and analyzed in real-time. However, uploading all the data to the cloud may incur high latency and network congestion, which can impact the performance of the application.

To address these challenges, the edge computing paradigm is proposed, where data processing and analysis are performed at the edge of the network, closer to the data source, rather than in the cloud \cite{arxivID-2102.02720-1, arxivID-2107.06835-2}. Edge computing has gained significant attention in recent years due to its potential to provide low-latency and high-performance computing services. It enables the deployment of applications and services at the network edge, closer to the data sources, reducing the need for data transfer to the cloud \cite{arxivID-1702.05309-3, arxivID-1909.08747-4}. By performing computation at the edge, edge computing can reduce the communication overhead and latency, and improve the overall performance of the system \cite{arxivID-2111.10076-5}. Edge computing is expected to play a crucial role in the future of computing, particularly in the context of IoT and 5G networks, where the demand for low-latency and high-performance computing services is increasing \cite{arxivID-1912.00595-6, arxivID-2107.13374-7}.

Edge computing has been widely applied in various domains, such as industrial IoT, smart cities, smart transportation, and smart healthcare \cite{arxivID-1911.11966-8, arxivID-1909.08747-9, arxivID-1712.04135-10, arxivID-2004.07311-11, arxivID-2103.12144-12}. For example, in the industrial IoT, edge computing enables real-time data processing and analysis, allowing for faster decision-making and improved operational efficiency \cite{arxivID-2005.11146-13}. In smart cities, edge computing can enable intelligent traffic management and real-time monitoring of public safety \cite{arxivID-2109.03141-14, arxivID-2103.01506-15}. In smart transportation, edge computing can enable real-time traffic monitoring and management, as well as autonomous driving \cite{arxivID-1907.09454-16, arxivID-2012.10252-17}. In smart healthcare, edge computing can enable real-time patient monitoring and remote diagnosis, allowing for faster response times and improved patient outcomes \cite{arxivID-2301.10009-18}

