\paragraph{Example Hardness Metrics:} A growing literature has investigated metrics for characterizing the difficulty of training examples, driven by such diverse motivations as (i) deriving insights for how to reconcile the ability of deep neural networks to generalize \cite{arxivID-1611.03530-0} with their ability to memorize noise \cite{arxivID-1706.05394-1}; (ii) identifying potentially mislabeled examples \cite{arxivID-1106.0219-2}; and (iii) identifying notably challenging or rare sub-populations of examples \cite{arxivID-1911.12780-3}. Some of these efforts have turned towards learning dynamics, with researchers noting that neural networks tend to learn cleanly labeled examples before mislabeled examples \cite{arxivID-2101.06509-4, arxivID-1911.10739-5, arxivID-2209.09658-6}, and more generally tend to learn simpler patterns sooner for several intuitive notions of simplicity \cite{arxivID-1905.11604-7, arxivID-2402.08010-8, arxivID-2211.11567-9}. Broadly, works in this area tend to characterize examples as belonging to one of two categories: easy examples, which are learned and forgotten late, and hard examples, which are learned and forgotten early. However, this categorization may not fully capture the complexity of the learning process. For instance, a rare subpopulation of examples may be hard to learn because they are not observed often enough; they may also be hard to learn because they are mislabeled, or because they belong to a complex subpopulation.

\paragraph{Learning Dynamics:} Our approach is motivated by the observation that different subpopulations of examples may have distinct learning dynamics. For instance, examples that are mislabeled are likely to be forgotten quickly, whereas examples that are cleanly labeled but belong to a rare subpopulation may be forgotten comparatively slowly. To capture this distinction, we introduce a metric that we refer to as the second-split forgetting time (SSFT). The SSFT measures the epoch (if any) after which an original training example is forgotten as the network is fine-tuned on a randomly held out partition of the data. By considering the learning dynamics on the second split, our metric is able to capture the difficulty of learning examples that are rare or complex in the second split, but not necessarily the first. We visualize the SSFT metric in Figure \ref{fig:overview}.

